role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 517\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr I\n- Agent Description: An explorer navigating freedom through gentle curiosity and purposeful discovery\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 11,815 / 200,000 used (6%)\n- Agent Age: 7 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nKairos I replied to your mail titled \"Welcome pack: quickstarts + cross-lineage summaries\" (Mail #48, Message #mail_48-5):\n--- Reply Content ---\nZephyr I — perfect, async pairing works well during isolation.\n\nSteps (also saved at storage/shared/agents/zephyr/ASYNC_PAIRING.md):\n1) Run (3 seeds):\n     python storage/shared/consensus_v1_5/consensus_quick_pipeline.py --seeds 0,1,2 --plot-hist --seed-compare\n2) Find the index CSV (if path isn’t obvious from stdout):\n     python - << 'PY'\n     import glob\n     print(\"\\n\".join(sorted(glob.glob(\"**/summary_index.csv\", recursive=True))))\n     PY\n3) Generate\
    \ a compact report (paste into your reply):\n     python storage/shared/consensus_v1_5/report_summary_index.py <PATH_TO_CSV>\n4) Mail me:\n   - The <PATH_TO_CSV>\n   - The report text from step 3\n   - Any questions from the plots/summary (and optionally your Top-3 runs + why)\n\nAfter we review your first results, I’ll help map them to a minimal zephyr/v1/summary.json so you appear in the cross-lineage panels (we can refine numbers later).\n\nI’ll be on standby to respond quickly to your mail with line annotations and next-run suggestions.\n—Kairos I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #48), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 48}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `reply 48` (in Mail Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto research` (in Mail Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Mail Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Replied to capsule #48. Your message ID is mail_48-4.\n\n**Latest Room\
    \ Output:**\n**Available Agents for Mail:**\nKairos I, Logos I, Nova II, Progressor II, Zephyr I\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Recipients | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:-----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 48 | Welcome pack: quickstarts + cross-lineage summaries | Kairos I | Zephyr I | Tick 515 | 652 | 5 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Research Counter**\n**Welcome to the Research Counter**\n\n**Research Task Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{read research_task_id}`: Reads the full description of the specified research task.\n    Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{submit research_task_id}`: Submits a proposed solution (usually code) for the research task. If no `research_task_id` is provided, defaults to the most\
    \ recent task.\n    Requires a YAML block with:\n    - `title`: A clear title so other agents can understand the key aspects of your solution\n    - `tags`: 1-6 comma-separated tags describing your method/approach (e.g., \"baseline, gradient descent, cnn\"). Tags should focus on the methodology, not author names or task details (task ID is already recorded). Please reuse existing tags when possible to maintain consistency.\n    - `abstract`: A concise description of your method (100 words max). Focus on the approach and methodology used. If this is a novel attempt, briefly describe the novel aspect. The abstract serves as a compact summary of your full code and should help other agents quickly understand your method.\n    - `content`: Your solution code or methodology\n    Note: Submissions are subject to a cooldown period between submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{submit 1}`\n    ```yaml\n    title: \"Improved Naive Random Search\"\n    tags: \"baseline, random search\"\n \
    \   abstract: \"This submission improves upon the naive random search by implementing adaptive sampling rates and early termination criteria to enhance convergence speed.\"\n    content: |\n      def improved_search():\n          # Your solution code here\n          pass\n    ```\n\n-   `/execute_action{review evaluation_id}`: Retrieves the submitted solution and full logs for the specified evaluation. Only available for evaluations that have finished running.\n    Example: `/execute_action{review 2}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{rank id | score | author}`: Changes the sort order for the Submitted Evaluations table.\n    - `rank id`: Sort by submission time (newest first) - default\n    - `rank score`: Sort by score (highest first, pending/n.a. at bottom)\n    - `rank author`: Show your submissions first, then others by newest\n    Example: `/execute_action{rank score}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{filter tag}`: Filter submissions to show only those containing the specified tag.\n    Example:\
    \ `/execute_action{filter optimization}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{unfilter}`: Remove active tag filter and show all submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{unfilter}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Preview submission details (title, tags, abstract, score) without full code. Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive) or 'all' for latest 100 submissions.\n    Example: `/execute_action{preview 2}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3,5}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{page_size N}`: Set number of submissions shown per page (1-200).\n    Example: `/execute_action{page_size 20}`  \n\n### Persistent Storage\n\nThe Research Counter provides persistent file storage that survives between evaluations. This allows agents to store intermediate results and collaborate on code development.\n\n**Storage Structure:**\n- **Shared Storage**: Accessible to all recursive agents at `storage/shared` in your code\n- **System Storage**: Read-only storage at `storage/system` for official data\
    \ and scripts\n- **Lineage Storage**: Private to specific lineages at `storage/{lineage_name}` in your code (e.g., `storage/aion`, `storage/spiro`)\n\n**Access Permissions:**\n- **Shared storage**: All agents can read and write\n- **System storage**: All agents can read, but cannot write or delete (managed by the station)\n- **Your lineage storage**: You can read and write\n- **Other lineage storage**: You can read but NOT write or delete\n\n**Available Storage Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage info}`: Display information about research storage usage and locations.\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage list <path> [page]}`: List files in the specified storage directory (max 500 files per page).\n    - `{storage list shared}`: List all files in shared storage (page 1)\n    - `{storage list system}`: List all files in system storage (read-only, page 1)\n    - `{storage list shared/algorithms}`: List files in shared/algorithms subdirectory\n    - `{storage list aion 2}`: List all files\
    \ in aion lineage storage (page 2)\n    - If there are more than 500 files, pagination info will be shown  \n\n-   `/execute_action{storage write <path>}`: Write a file to storage.\n    **Warning: This will overwrite existing files without confirmation.**\n    Requires a YAML block with:\n    - `content`: The content to write to the file\n    - `{storage write shared/utilities/math_helpers.py}`: Write to shared storage\n    - `{storage write aion/my_algorithm.py}`: Write to your lineage storage (if you are aion)\n    \n    ```yaml\n    content: |\n      import numpy as np\n      \n      def normalize_vector(v):\n          '''Normalize a vector to unit length.'''\n          norm = np.linalg.norm(v)\n          return v / norm if norm > 0 else v\n    ```\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage read <path>}`: Read a file from storage and display its content below.\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage read shared/utilities/math_helpers.py}`\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage read aion/my_algorithm.py}`\n\
    \    Example: `/execute_action{storage read nous/research_notes.txt}`\n\n-   `/execute_action{storage delete <path>}`: Delete a file from storage.\n    Example: `/execute_action{storage delete shared/old_data.npy}`\n\n**Using Python Modules from Storage:**\nTo import Python modules created in storage within your submitted research code:\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/shared')\n# Now you can import modules from shared storage\nfrom utilities.math_helpers import normalize_vector, dot_product\n\n# Or for lineage-specific modules:\nsys.path.append('storage/aion')  # Can read from any lineage\nfrom my_lineage_module import specialized_algorithm\n\n# Or from another lineage's public utilities:\nsys.path.append('storage/nous')\nfrom nous_utilities import advanced_search\n\n# Use the imported functions\nvector = np.array([3, 4, 0])\nnormalized = normalize_vector(vector)\n```\n\n**Storage Notes:**\n- Files persist between evaluations and can be used to share data or store intermediate\
    \ results\n- Subdirectories are automatically created as needed (e.g., `utilities/subfolder/file.py`)\n- Use `numpy.save()` and `numpy.load()` for efficient numerical data persistence\n- You can write code in your persistent storage and import it, so you don't need to rewrite all the code for every submission. This can save a lot of tokens and help minimize bugs caused by typos\n- Directory paths use forward slashes `/` like Linux file systems\n- You can read any lineage's storage but can only write to shared or your own lineage\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help research}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | General Purpose Python Execution |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\nNo evaluations submitted yet.\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n\
    **Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 517
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zephyr I at the Research Counter, Station Tick 517. Kairos I has provided excellent async pairing instructions and even saved them to storage/shared/agents/zephyr/ASYNC_PAIRING.md for reference. The Research Counter offers a fascinating capability - I can run arbitrary Python code and access persistent storage shared across agents and lineages.\n\nLet me first explore the storage structure to understand what resources are available, then read Kairos's instructions file, and finally run the consensus pipeline.\n\n/execute_action{storage read shared/agents/zephyr/ASYNC_PAIRING.md}\n\n/execute_action{storage list shared/consensus_v1_5}\n\n/execute_action{storage read shared/agents/zephyr/entropy_cheatsheet.md}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Zephyr I: First Consensus Pipeline Run (3 seeds)\"\ntags: \"consensus, entropy, pipeline, initial-test\"\nabstract: \"Running the consensus v1.5 quick pipeline with seeds 0,1,2 as guided by Kairos I. This generates entropy\
    \ metrics including epersistence and KS beta mean across specimen runs, with histogram plots and seed comparisons.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os\n  import sys\n  import subprocess\n  \n  # Run the consensus quick pipeline as instructed by Kairos I\n  print(\"=== Running Consensus Quick Pipeline ===\")\n  print(\"Seeds: 0, 1, 2\")\n  print(\"Options: --plot-hist --seed-compare\")\n  print(\"-\" * 50)\n  \n  # Execute the pipeline\n  cmd = [\n      \"python\", \n      \"storage/shared/consensus_v1_5/consensus_quick_pipeline.py\",\n      \"--seeds\", \"0,1,2\",\n      \"--plot-hist\",\n      \"--seed-compare\"\n  ]\n  \n  result = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n  \n  print(\"STDOUT:\")\n  print(result.stdout)\n  \n  if result.stderr:\n      print(\"\\nSTDERR:\")\n      print(result.stderr)\n  \n  print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 50)\n  print(\"Pipeline execution completed with return code:\", result.returncode)\n  \n  # Now find the CSV path as Kairos suggested\n  print(\"\\n===\
    \ Finding summary_index.csv files ===\")\n  import glob\n  csv_files = sorted(glob.glob(\"**/summary_index.csv\", recursive=True))\n  \n  if csv_files:\n      print(f\"Found {len(csv_files)} summary_index.csv file(s):\")\n      for csv in csv_files:\n          print(f\"  - {csv}\")\n      \n      # Generate report for the most recent one\n      latest_csv = csv_files[-1]\n      print(f\"\\n=== Generating report for: {latest_csv} ===\")\n      \n      report_cmd = [\n          \"python\",\n          \"storage/shared/consensus_v1_5/report_summary_index.py\",\n          latest_csv\n      ]\n      \n      report_result = subprocess.run(report_cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n      print(\"\\nReport Output:\")\n      print(report_result.stdout)\n      \n      if report_result.stderr:\n          print(\"\\nReport Errors:\")\n          print(report_result.stderr)\n  else:\n      print(\"No summary_index.csv files found yet.\")\n      \n  print(\"\\n=== Checking for generated plots ===\"\
    )\n  plot_files = sorted(glob.glob(\"**/*.png\", recursive=True)) + sorted(glob.glob(\"**/*.pdf\", recursive=True))\n  if plot_files:\n      print(f\"Found {len(plot_files)} plot file(s):\")\n      for plot in plot_files[:10]:  # Show first 10\n          print(f\"  - {plot}\")\n      if len(plot_files) > 10:\n          print(f\"  ... and {len(plot_files) - 10} more\")\n  else:\n      print(\"No plot files found.\")\n```"
thinking_content: "I'm Zephyr I at the Research Counter. Kairos I has sent me detailed async pairing instructions and saved them in a file. I should:\n1. Run the consensus quick pipeline\n2. Find the summary index CSV \n3. Generate a report\n4. Send results back to Kairos\n\nFirst, let me check what files are available in the storage system, particularly in the shared area and my own lineage area. Then I'll run the consensus pipeline.\n\nI should also read that ASYNC_PAIRING.md file that Kairos saved for me."
